window.docContents[21].resolve({"/Elliptic-Equations/Coverage-and-Open-Problems/#PDEAtlas--Elliptic-Equations--Coverage-and-Open-Problems--Elliptic-Coverage-and-Open-Problems--Fundamental-solutions-and-classical-harmonic-functions--Maximum-principles-and-energy-identities":{"contents":"The weak maximum and minimum principles, comparison, and Dirichlet uniqueness\nare proved for Euclidean balls and functions continuous on the closed ball.\nThe strong maximum and minimum principles are proved for harmonic functions on\narbitrary open preconnected sets.\n\nThe variable-coefficient theory treats\n. Its weak maximum\nprinciple assumes an open domain with compact closure, nonnegative principal\nquadratic forms, a uniform positive lower bound on one diagonal entry, an\nupper bound on the corresponding drift component, and .\nA boundary bound  with  controls a classical subsolution\ncontinuous on the closure.\n\nThe strong principle uses local uniform ellipticity and local upper bounds\non the trace, drift norm, and nonnegative potential. An attained maximum\n with  forces constancy on an open preconnected domain.\nBoth matrix principles allow arbitrary real principal matrices: their\nself-adjoint parts give the same operator on  functions.\nCoefficient continuity is not required.\n\nThe Hopf lemma for the same operators gives a strictly positive outer normal\nderivative at a boundary maximum point with an interior ball condition. A\nHarnack inequality is proved for nonnegative  solutions of\n with continuous bounded coefficients, symmetric uniformly elliptic\nprincipal part, and a potential of either sign, through a contact-set measure\nestimate and geometric decay of superlevel sets.\nRadial symmetry of positive solutions of  on the unit ball\nwith zero boundary values is proved by moving planes, for  solutions\nand globally Lipschitz .\n\nGreen's first and second identities are proved on coordinate boxes with their\nboundary fluxes expanded face by face. The resulting classical Dirichlet\nuniqueness theorem assumes pointwise  data on a nondegenerate closed\nbox. This is separate from weak uniqueness in , where the boundary\ncondition and integration by parts are encoded variationally. On a bounded open\nset with  graph boundary and forcing continuous in , the\nclassical Dirichlet principle\nidentifies the solutions of  in the classical boundary class with\nthe minimizers of the Dirichlet energy over its finite-energy members.\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Elliptic Equations\u0009Coverage and Open Problems\u0009Elliptic Coverage and Open Problems\u0009Fundamental solutions and classical harmonic functions","header":"5.7.1.1.3. Maximum principles and energy identities","id":"/Elliptic-Equations/Coverage-and-Open-Problems/#PDEAtlas--Elliptic-Equations--Coverage-and-Open-Problems--Elliptic-Coverage-and-Open-Problems--Fundamental-solutions-and-classical-harmonic-functions--Maximum-principles-and-energy-identities"},"/Foundations/Coordinates-and-Boundary-Calculus/#PDEAtlas--Foundations--Coordinates-and-Boundary-Calculus--Boundary-Calculus-and-C___-Boundary-Geometry--Chart-parameter-measures-and-physical-chart-measures":{"contents":"The Jacobian-weighted measure on the flat parameter space isThe ofReal conversion turns the nonnegative real Jacobian into an\nextended-nonnegative density.\n\nWritten as a density, the measure is convenient for measure-theoretic\nmanipulation, but concrete computations want the Jacobian as an integrand. The\ntwo viewpoints agree because  is a nonnegative real number, so\nconverting it to an extended-nonnegative density and back loses nothing.\n\nFor a function  into a real normed vector space,In Lean, multiplication of the real Jacobian with a vector-valued integrand\nis scalar multiplication.\n\nUnfold the restricted measure and withDensity. The Bochner integral against\nan extended-nonnegative density uses its real value. Since normDet is\nnonnegative, toReal (ofReal J_i^\\partial)=J_i^\\partial, yielding the stated\nweighted set integral.\n\nThe parameter measure lives on the flat space . Transporting it\nalong  produces a measure on physical space which, by construction,\nis the parametrized area of the boundary piece seen by chart .\n\nThe physical surface measure contributed by chart  is the pushforwardThis measure is concentrated on the physical chart boundary piece\n: almost every point\nbelongs to that piece. Its topological support can also contain limit points\noutside the open chart source.\n\nFor a pushforward measure the Bochner integral formula needs measurability\nonly with respect to the pushforward itself, which is weaker than strong\nmeasurability of  on all of .\n\nLet  be a real normed vector space and let\n. If  is almost everywhere strongly measurable\nwith respect to the specific chart measure , then\n\nUse the Bochner integral formula for a mapped measure to pull the integral\nback through . Then apply the parameter-measure integral formula to\n.\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Foundations\u0009Coordinates and Boundary Calculus\u0009Boundary Calculus and C¹ Boundary Geometry","header":"3.3.2.10. Chart parameter measures and physical chart measures","id":"/Foundations/Coordinates-and-Boundary-Calculus/#PDEAtlas--Foundations--Coordinates-and-Boundary-Calculus--Boundary-Calculus-and-C___-Boundary-Geometry--Chart-parameter-measures-and-physical-chart-measures"},"/Foundations/Coordinates-and-Boundary-Calculus/#PDEAtlas--Foundations--Coordinates-and-Boundary-Calculus--Boundary-Calculus-and-C___-Boundary-Geometry--Compatibility-of-chart-measures":{"contents":"For any extended-nonnegative integrand\n,The formal statement uses ENNReal.ofReal on each Jacobian and lintegral,\nso it does not need an integrability assumption.\n\nApply the same-dimensional change-of-variables theorem to the injective\n mapThe image theorem identifies its image exactly with the reverse overlap.\nThe chain rule and\n identify the derivative of the composite.\nMultiplicativity of normDet converts the transition Jacobian times\n into . Substitute these identities into the\nchange-of-variables formula.\n\nThe transition identity compares two Jacobian-weighted parameter integrals. To\nturn it into a statement about the physical measures  and\n, each side must first be recognized as an integral\nagainst one chart measure restricted to the other chart's source.\n\nIf  is measurable, then restricting chart\n's physical measure to chart 's source gives\n\nUnfold the pushforward measure and pull the restricted lintegral back\nthrough . The preimage of chart 's source inside\n is precisely . Unfold the parameter\nmeasure's Jacobian density to obtain the right-hand side.\n\nCombining the two formulas, the restriction\n integrates\n over , its mirror image integrates over\n, and the transition identity equates the two. Applied to\nindicator functions, this is equality of measures.\n\nThe two chart measures agree on their common physical patch:\n\nUse measure extensionality. For a measurable set , represent its measure\nas the lintegral of the indicator of . Rewrite each restricted chart\nmeasure by its parameter-overlap formula. The transition integral theorem\nidentifies the two Jacobian-weighted parameter integrals.\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Foundations\u0009Coordinates and Boundary Calculus\u0009Boundary Calculus and C¹ Boundary Geometry","header":"3.3.2.11. Compatibility of chart measures","id":"/Foundations/Coordinates-and-Boundary-Calculus/#PDEAtlas--Foundations--Coordinates-and-Boundary-Calculus--Boundary-Calculus-and-C___-Boundary-Geometry--Compatibility-of-chart-measures"},"/Hyperbolic-Equations/Propagation___-shocks___-and-weak-selection/#PDEAtlas--Hyperbolic-Equations--Propagation___-shocks___-and-weak-selection--The-affine-Burgers-solution___-step-by-step":{"contents":"Burgers's equation with affine data  is solved by\n for as long as the denominator does not vanish. The\ndenominator is also the derivative of the characteristic position with respect\nto its initial footpoint. Its nonvanishing is therefore the geometric\ncondition that lets us recover a unique footpoint from the current position.\nThe division in the formula and the invertibility of characteristics carry\nthe same restriction.\n\nThe affine-solution theorem\nfollows by differentiating on  and checking that\n:\n\nopen BurgersEquation PDE.Common in\nopen PDE (SpaceTime partialDeriv) in\nexample (a c : ℝ) (x : SpaceTime 1) (hden : burgersDenom a x ≠ 0) :\n    partialDeriv (0 : Fin 2) (burgersAffineSolution a c) x =\n      -a * burgersNumer a c x * (burgersDenom a x)⁻¹ * (burgersDenom a x)⁻¹ := by\n  unfold burgersAffineSolution burgersNumer burgersInvDenom burgersDenom\n  unfold burgersDenom at hden\n  cas\n\n\nopen BurgersEquation PDE.Common in\nopen PDE (SpaceTime partialDeriv) in\nexample (a c : ℝ) (x : SpaceTime 1) (hden : burgersDenom a x ≠ 0) :\n    partialDeriv (1 : Fin 2) (burgersAffineSolution a c) x =\n      a * (burgersDenom a x)⁻¹ := by\n  unfold burgersAffineSolution burgersNumer burgersInvDenom burgersDenom\n  unfold burgersDenom at hden\n  cas\n\n\nopen BurgersEquation PDE.Common in\nopen PDE (SpaceTime partialDeriv) in\nexample (a c : ℝ) (x : SpaceTime 1) (hden : burgersDenom a x ≠ 0) :\n    partialDeriv (0 : Fin 2) (burgersAffineSolution a c) x +\n        burgersAffineSolution a c x *\n          partialDeriv (1 : Fin 2) (burgersAffineSolution a c) x = 0 := by\n  unfold burgersAffineSolution burgersNumer burgersInvDenom burgersDenom\n  unfold burgersDenom at hden\n  cas\n\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Hyperbolic Equations\u0009Propagation, shocks, and weak selection","header":"7.1.4. The affine Burgers solution, step by step","id":"/Hyperbolic-Equations/Propagation___-shocks___-and-weak-selection/#PDEAtlas--Hyperbolic-Equations--Propagation___-shocks___-and-weak-selection--The-affine-Burgers-solution___-step-by-step"},"/Sobolev-Spaces-and-Weak-Derivatives/Extension___-Trace___-and-Embeddings/#PDEAtlas--Sobolev-Spaces-and-Weak-Derivatives--Extension___-Trace___-and-Embeddings--Extension-and-Density-on-Regular-Domains--Operational-C___-Geometry":{"contents":"Evans's first-order extension construction is carried out from explicit\nfinite boundary data.\n\nThe local idea is reflection. After flattening a boundary patch, reflect the\nfunction across the coordinate hyperplane, then return to the original\ncoordinates. The chain rule and change of variables explain why derivative\nbounds for both the chart and its inverse enter the norm estimate. Cutoffs\nkeep each reflected piece within its chart, and a finite partition of unity\njoins the pieces to the unchanged interior part. Later boundary estimates\nreuse the same localization, with stronger chart regularity when more\nderivatives are required.\n\nFor positive dimension, an operational  extension atlas on\n contains:* proofs that  is open and bounded;* a finite number  of boundary charts;* for each , an open partial homeomorphism of\n;* globally defined  representatives of the chart map and inverse map;* the local equivalence\n on the chart\nsource;* boundedness of each chart target;* one smooth compactly supported interior cutoff supported in ;* finitely many smooth compactly supported boundary cutoffs, each supported in\nits chart source;* the pointwise partition identityThe partial homeomorphism is required to be invertible only between its open\nsource and target. The total functions used to represent it are globally\n because the weak chain rule and pullback theorems are stated for\nglobal maps.The class contains the finite atlas and partition of unity that the proofs\nuse. Every bounded open set with  graph boundary\nadmits these data.\n\nThe domainhas a fully constructed operational atlas. The left chart is translation by\n; the right chart is reflection . On suitable bounded open\nchart sources these maps flatten the two endpoints to the positive half-line.\nA finite smooth partition of unity on the closure supplies one interior and\ntwo boundary cutoffs.The resulting c1ExtensionAtlas is installed as the typeclass instance\ndomainC1ExtensionAtlas. Thus all the extension, density, trace, and\ncompactness results formulated for an operational atlas can be instantiated on\nthis concrete nonempty interval without adding geometric axioms.\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Sobolev Spaces and Weak Derivatives\u0009Extension, Trace, and Embeddings\u0009Extension and Density on Regular Domains","header":"4.3.1.3. Operational C¹ Geometry","id":"/Sobolev-Spaces-and-Weak-Derivatives/Extension___-Trace___-and-Embeddings/#PDEAtlas--Sobolev-Spaces-and-Weak-Derivatives--Extension___-Trace___-and-Embeddings--Extension-and-Density-on-Regular-Domains--Operational-C___-Geometry"}});